<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Proposal 44 execution plan, C0 - THE PUPPET'S KINEMATIC CHAIN.

  WHY THIS EXISTS. The shipped puppet rotated the neck about the pelvis
  correctly and then built EVERY segment above it in WORLD coordinates from
  the neck's new position:

      headC     = rotateAbout( neck, QPointF( neck.x(), neck.y() - r ), nod );
      shoulderL = QPointF( neck.x() - shoulderW, neck.y() + torsoLen*0.06 );

  `(neck.x, neck.y - r)` is straight up on the SCREEN, not along the torso
  axis, so the head, the shoulder bar and both arms inherited the neck's
  TRANSLATION and none of its ROTATION. A torso at 20 degrees with a
  bolt-upright head and a perfectly level shoulder bar is a figure with no
  spine - the head reads as gimbal-mounted. That is the defect a user
  reported as "the head is not subordinate to the bending of the torso", and
  it is a DRAWING bug: it waits on no analysis change and no plant.

  WHY THE VERB TAKES EXPLICIT JOINT SCALARS. Nothing in this repo can drive
  an analysis-derived pose to a chosen angle: the pose is a pure function of
  decoded aspects, `assert-feel-flow-pose` only READS it, and no fixture can
  be authored to land `sway` on exactly 0.5 of full scale. So the gate
  supplies the geometry's own inputs and asserts the geometry - a unit gate
  reached through the verb. The painter and this verb call ONE function
  (`sFeelFlowSkeletonFor`), which is the discipline proposal 41 M7 paid for
  with `tagChipRect()` and the take-lane fix paid for again with
  `fillBodyByMaterial()`: two implementations of one layout drift, and they
  drift silently.

  THE ANGLES ARE RECOVERED FROM THE POINTS, never echoed back from the
  inputs that produced them. An assertion against a number the function was
  TOLD would be tautological; one recovered from the resulting geometry
  fails when the construction is wrong.

  MEASURED (first run, 2026-08-28, this box, Qt 6.4.2 / Ubuntu 24.04),
  default 200x400 box:

    sway= 0.0            trunk   0.0000  headStub   0.0000  bar   0.0000  armL   0.0000  armR   0.0000
    sway= 0.5            trunk  10.0000  headStub  10.0000  bar  10.0000  armL  10.0000  armR  10.0000
    sway= 1.0            trunk  20.0000  headStub  20.0000  bar  20.0000  armL  20.0000  armR  20.0000
    sway=-0.5            trunk -10.0000  headStub -10.0000  bar -10.0000  armL -10.0000  armR -10.0000
    sway=-1.0            trunk -20.0000  headStub -20.0000  bar -20.0000  armL -20.0000  armR -20.0000
    sway=1 nod=1 arm=1   trunk  20.0000  headStub  30.0000  bar  20.0000  armL  55.0000  armR -15.0000

  The last row is what proves the composition is a SUM and not a
  replacement: 20+10 for the head's own nod, 20+35 and 20-35 for the two
  arms in antiphase. And the same angles come out of a 90x140 box, so the
  identity is scale-free - it is a rotation, not a pixel coincidence.

  WATCHED FAILING. Reverted `sFeelFlowSkeletonFor` to compose the head,
  shoulders and arms against 0 instead of the trunk angle (i.e. the shipped
  world-coordinate construction): every `inheritTol` row FAILS, reporting
  headStub/bar/armL/armR at 0.0000 against a trunk of +-10 or +-20. The
  sway=0 row still passes, as it must - at zero lean the broken and the
  correct construction coincide, which is exactly why the case sweeps five
  angles rather than asserting one.

  NOT GATED. What the figure LOOKS like (aesthetics are not gated anywhere
  in this feature); the head still follows the trunk RIGIDLY, with no neck
  lag, no counter-rotation and no inertia - that is C4's `m*d*a_base` term
  and C0 deliberately does not promise it; and the pose numbers themselves,
  which C0 does not touch (`feel_flow_puppet.qxa` still pins those).
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="feel_flow_puppet_chain">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- (1) THE CHAIN. With the head's own nod and the arms' own swing at
         zero, every segment above the neck must carry EXACTLY the trunk's
         lean. Five angles including zero and both signs: at sway=0 the
         broken construction and the correct one agree, so a single-angle
         assertion could not tell them apart. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="0"    nod="0" arm="0" expectTrunk="0"   inheritTol="0.01"/>
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="0.5"  nod="0" arm="0" expectTrunk="10"  inheritTol="0.01"/>
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="1.0"  nod="0" arm="0" expectTrunk="20"  inheritTol="0.01"/>
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="-0.5" nod="0" arm="0" expectTrunk="-10" inheritTol="0.01"/>
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="-1.0" nod="0" arm="0" expectTrunk="-20" inheritTol="0.01"/>

    <!-- (2) THE COMPOSITION IS A SUM, in the plane each DOF acts in. The
         head's nod is lateral like the trunk's lean, so it ADDS: 20 + 10.
         The arms are SAGITTAL since the model went 3D, so they no longer
         change armL/armR at all - those stay at the trunk's inherited 20 -
         and appear in armSwingL/R instead. On the 2D model this row read
         armL=55 armR=-15, which was the jumping-jack plane. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="1.0" nod="1.0" arm="1.0"
                            expectTrunk="20"
                            contains="headStub=30.0000 shoulderBar=20.0000 armL=20.0000 armR=20.0000"/>

    <!-- (2b) 36.6915, NOT 35. The arm is commanded 35 degrees in the TRUNK's
         frame and the trunk is also leaning 20 laterally, so its world-space
         sagittal angle is atan2(sin35, cos35*cos20) = 36.6915. A model that
         treated the two rotations as independent 2D angles would read exactly
         35 here, so this one number is the difference between a 3D body and
         two flat drawings stacked. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="1.0" arm="1.0" expectSwingL="36.6915" tol="0.001"/>

    <!-- (3) THE THREE ANATOMICAL AXES ARE DISTINCT. This is what "technically
         3D" has to mean: each rotation moves its own plane and leaves the
         other two alone. The shipped model had only the first row, and the
         requester's stated core interaction - bending FORWARD - is the
         second, which a front-facing 2D figure foreshortens to nothing. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="1.0"  expectTrunk="20" expectFlex="0"  expectTwist="0"/>
    <assert-puppet-skeleton flex="1.0"  expectTrunk="0"  expectFlex="25" expectTwist="0"/>
    <assert-puppet-skeleton twist="1.0" expectTrunk="0"  expectFlex="0"  expectTwist="-20"/>

    <!-- (3b) AND THEY COMPOSE AS ROTATIONS, NOT AS NUMBERS. Lateral 20 plus
         sagittal 25 does NOT give 20 and 25: it gives 21.8802 and 25.0000,
         with a twist of -8.7447 that neither command asked for. That
         cross-term is the signature of real rotation composition, and it is
         pinned precisely because a componentwise implementation would read a
         clean 20 / 25 / 0 and look plausible. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="1.0" flex="1.0"
                            expectTrunk="21.8802" expectFlex="25.0000"
                            expectTwist="-8.7447" tol="0.001"/>

    <!-- (3c) The arms hang from the trunk, so a FORWARD lean carries them
         forward with it even with no swing commanded. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton flex="1.0" arm="0" expectSwingL="25.0" tol="0.001"/>

    <!-- (3) SCALE-FREE. The same lean out of a box less than half the size
         in both axes - it is a rotation, not a pixel coincidence. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="0.5" nod="0" arm="0" boxW="90" boxH="140"
                            expectTrunk="10" inheritTol="0.01"/>

    <!-- (4) THE DEGENERATE BOX still answers, and answers INVALID rather
         than dividing by a zero-height figure. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="1.0" boxW="4" boxH="4" expectValid="0" contains="valid=0"/>
  </actions>
</SActionScript>
